WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.807 --> 00:00:08.140
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.720
It's a pleasure to be here
at Next with all of you.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:11.850
I'm Prajakta.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.938
I'm a product manager
in cloud networking.

00:00:13.938 --> 00:00:15.730
MIKE COLUMBUS: Yeah,
thanks for being here.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:16.750
My name's Mike Columbus.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.463
I manage a specialist team
of networking professionals

00:00:19.463 --> 00:00:21.630
that help customers like
yourselves on their journey

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.677
to Google Cloud.

00:00:23.677 --> 00:00:25.260
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
what does it really

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.700
take to build and manage
services in today's world?

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:30.960
The world is hybrid.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.300
The world is multi-cloud.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.150
Customers have hybrid and
multi-cloud deployments

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.430
because they want
higher availability,

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.070
they want to mix and match the
best of breed solutions from

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.890
each of the clouds, or
because they cannot move all

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.380
of their services to cloud.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.570
Now, there's no doubt that
hybrid and multi-cloud

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.680
deployments are here to stay.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.240
But one of the questions we
hear all the time from customers

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.350
is how can we go and manage
these deployments seamlessly

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.590
without all of the toil?

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.330
One of the big
trends that we see

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.030
is the move from monoliths
to microservices.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.850
And they're moving
to them for agility

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.130
and then also for manageability.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.830
But imagine you had a
monolith, you took it,

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.860
you chopped it up into
120 microservices.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.470
How are you going to deploy,
secure, connect, manage

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.710
these microservices?

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:27.780
What you really need is a way to
create new services uniformly.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.700
You also need services
management infrastructure,

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.020
which you obviously don't want
to create, build, or manage.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.290
And you need security,
resiliency, and observability

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.790
for your services.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.250
And that is where
Service Mesh comes in.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.820
Now, Service Mesh is a
very powerful abstraction

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.530
that's becoming
increasingly popular

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.630
to deliver
microservices and also

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.990
for multi-cloud applications.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.670
So think a few years back.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.980
Like, what exactly
is Service Mesh?

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.360
Think of boxes, appliances
a few years back.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.120
You had all of these
complex network appliances.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.740
What did you do to them?

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.040
Then came software-defined
networking.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.340
And you took this complex
box, you dis-aggregated it.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.130
You created a very
simple forwarding plane.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:15.780
And you moved all
of the complexity

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.580
up to the control plane.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:18.990
And then these
simple data planes

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.540
were controlled by
that control plane.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.270
Think of Service Mesh as
exactly SDN for services.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.000
Now, what are you doing here?

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.520
You're taking a
complex application.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.250
You're removing all of the
networking code from it.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.790
You're putting a lot of
it as a sidecar proxy.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.770
And then you need
something to go manage

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.378
all of these sidecar proxies.

00:02:39.378 --> 00:02:41.670
And that is where the control
plane of the Service Mesh

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.670
comes into the picture.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.540
So let's take a closer
look at the Service Mesh.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.040
What is a mesh?

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.710
Essentially, a
mesh abstracts away

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:53.960
the network from
your application

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.480
so your individual services
or the apps you write

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.000
don't necessarily have to
be aware of the network.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.630
They only know about
their local sidecar proxy.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:06.110
Now, each proxy obviously has
to be configured and managed.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.930
And so that's where the Service
Mesh control plane comes in.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.090
It takes all of these
distributed proxies

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.640
and configures them
into something larger.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.020
And then you get visibility.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.490
You get resiliency.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.980
You get traffic control
security and observability.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:23.440
So that is actually the value
add of your Service Mesh.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.180
Think of the data plane.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.340
We talked about a sidecar proxy.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.040
One of the most popular
sidecar proxies is Envoy.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.420
It was built at Lyft.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.980
It is a very high
performance proxy.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.000
It has [? eventually ?]
consistent service discovery.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.460
And the reason we like
is it's extensible.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.170
And it has a protocol
called xDS v2,

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.650
which it uses between the
control plane and the proxy.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.620
And so you could use
any other proxy that

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.510
supports this xDS protocol.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:52.220
And therefore, you can use
Envoy that Istio provides.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.280
You can use on Envoy
that is your own.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:55.840
Or you could use
another proxy that

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.900
is compliant with
this xDS v2 protocol.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.400
So it's fairly--
it doesn't lock you

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.580
in into any specific proxy
or even any specific control

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.690
plane.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.720
So the biggest benefit
of Service Mesh

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.780
is that it decouples
development from operations.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:12.730
And so as a developer,
you don't need

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.650
to worry about writing
and maintaining

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:19.670
policies and the networking code
inside your application at all.

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.060
One of the really nice
things about Service Mesh

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.992
is it doesn't make
any assumptions.

00:04:23.992 --> 00:04:26.200
Now, it doesn't make
assumptions whether your service

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.330
is in cloud, on-prem.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.910
It doesn't make assumptions
about whether your service is

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.340
a VM-based service, a
container-based service.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.440
So it assumes things
are heterogeneous.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.390
It assumes they are spread out.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:40.120
And that is what it affords
you in that it gives you

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:43.420
this unifying abstraction to
create services and obviously

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.010
manage them at scale.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.970
So one of the most popular
Service Mesh technologies,

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.820
which is an open
source, is Istio.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:53.140
What is Istio?

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.850
It's essentially giving
you three main things--

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.330
one is advanced networking
capabilities and routing

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.220
capabilities-- but without you
having to write that code--

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:04.510
enhanced security,
and it is also

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.700
giving you a lot
of observability

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.430
into what is happening as
your services communicate

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.880
with each other.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.130
Let's take a closer
look at Istio.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.900
Now, look at the data plane.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.940
That is where you
have your service.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.520
Sitting next to it
is a sidecar proxy.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.480
And then on the top
is the control plane.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.180
So it has three main pieces--

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.670
pilot, mixer, and then there's
the Istio security bit.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:30.000
Pilot is going to give
Envoys the configuration.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.990
It's going to do
the load balancing.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.740
And then it's also going
to do service discovery.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.330
What you have on
the security side

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.360
is things such as mutual
TLS between your services.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.750
And then observability lets you
map out all of your services

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.070
along with the traffic flow.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.737
And then other things
that are happening

00:05:45.737 --> 00:05:49.530
is your services
communicate with each other.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.840
Now, one of the other
things that you would also

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.390
need for your Service
Mesh is you need something

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.070
that guards your entry point.

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.770
And that is Istio
gateway or your ingress.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.920
And now, this is where you would
essentially deploy something

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.170
like a global load
balancer in front.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.750
The reason is that you
need the DDoS defense.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.270
You would put your Istio
gateway on instances behind.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.800
And then that becomes your
entry point to the mesh.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:13.290
So if you think of
it, you've taken

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.110
all of the traffic routing,
all of the customization,

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.990
all of the programmability right
to the edge of your service

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.870
mesh with this one.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:26.130
One of the key pieces
of Istio is pilot.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.930
This is the one that
goes and manages

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.870
and configures all
of the Envoy proxies.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.430
It is the one that
knows about all

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.620
of the other Envoy instances.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.840
It's the one that provides
service discovery.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.990
And it also delivers
advanced traffic control.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:43.720
Now, our customers
love Service Mesh.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.505
But they always say, we
don't want to go and build

00:06:46.505 --> 00:06:47.380
these control planes.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:50.090
We don't want to
go manage pilot.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:51.610
And so what did we do?

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:54.190
If you weren't there in
the keynote this morning,

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:56.630
we launched Traffic Director--
so you are one of the first

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:58.920
people to hear about
it after that launch--

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:03.220
our new GCP-managed service
for the Service Mesh.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:04.900
And what it really
does is it helps

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:10.000
you power up service meshes
of all types, of all sizes.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.250
You get global load
balancing, which is huge.

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:14.170
And we'll talk about
it a little bit more.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.670
You get centralized
health checking.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.903
So there is somebody that's
offloading your proxies

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:20.320
from health checking every
other application instance that

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:21.830
is in your network.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:23.620
You also get traffic
driven autoscaling.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:27.970
So as your traffic grows, your
services scale up and down.

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:30.670
And then you get traffic control
capabilities like canaries,

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:33.285
and so on that we'll talk
about a little bit more.

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:35.035
One of the nice things
is Traffic Director

00:07:35.035 --> 00:07:37.690
is GCP-managed,
which means you get

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.600
a production grade [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.880
And so if something happens,
you can call our support.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:44.630
You don't have to worry
about it yourself.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.310
So if you look at this,
you see Traffic Director

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:50.530
in the control plane.

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:53.380
It's managing the
sidecar proxies.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:55.630
One of the nice things
about Traffic Director

00:07:55.630 --> 00:08:00.020
is it works out of the box
for VMs and containers.

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:02.320
So if you look at
the service here,

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.690
imagine that your front-end
service is essentially

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:06.730
self-managed docker.

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.950
Your shopping cart
services deployed in VMs

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:11.590
because you possibly had
that service from before.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.990
And then your payment
service could be again in VMs

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:16.370
or it could be a GKE service.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:18.820
And so what this means
is you have a unified way

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.420
to do traffic management
for any type of service

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.760
without having to have to
have a separate implementation

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.800
or a solution for each
one of these types.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.520
Now, a lot of you here
are likely our global load

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.440
balancing customers.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.330
And one of the questions
we would always get

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.820
is when are you bringing
global load balancing

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:42.863
to microservices that are
internal, not external facing?

00:08:42.863 --> 00:08:44.280
And that is where
Traffic Director

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:46.250
is going to bring
global load balancing

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.620
to your internal microservices
and to the Service Mesh.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.150
And to take a closer
look at it, I'd

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.400
like to invite Mike to
give you a deep dive

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.370
into the global load
balancing aspect.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.325
MIKE COLUMBUS: Thanks, Prajakta.

00:08:58.325 --> 00:08:58.825
Good job.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:03.810
So in the next
section, I'm going

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:06.540
to talk about global load
balancing with Traffic Director

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:09.372
and also have the pleasure of
doing a few live demos in front

00:09:09.372 --> 00:09:10.330
of my new friends here.

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:13.840
So no pressure there.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:16.110
And then finally, we'll
take a deeper look

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:18.600
at what happened under the
covers when I ran these demos.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:20.460
We're going to taste
a few different views

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:21.340
into the environment.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.333
So it should be fun.

00:09:23.333 --> 00:09:24.750
But before we get
to that, I think

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:27.300
it's really important to
understand the data model

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.460
that Traffic Director uses.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:32.580
So I'm sure a lot of
folks in the audience

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:36.450
have deployed a load balancer
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.240
This data model
should not look--

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.850
it should look similar, right?

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:45.990
So the first thing that you need
is a global forwarding role.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:50.970
This consists of an IP,
a port, and a protocol.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:52.475
And what's a little
bit different

00:09:52.475 --> 00:09:53.850
about this global
forwarding role

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:56.130
is that the IP only
has significance

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.920
to the services when they try
to talk to other services inside

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.120
of the mesh.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.262
The IP can really
be anything, right?

00:10:02.262 --> 00:10:03.720
And I'll get into
a little bit more

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:06.650
about how that works later.

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:07.650
So think of it this way.

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:10.530
For every service inside the
mesh that needs a unique VIP,

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.510
you're going to need
a global forwarding

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:15.150
role for that service.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.900
Global forwarding rules
point to target HTTP proxies.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.410
A good way to think about these
are the actual service proxies

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.180
inside of your mesh, right?

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:25.920
So this is really powerful.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.160
These proxies are the ones that
are getting traffic redirected

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.280
to it and creating the
policy that's distributed

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.050
throughout the mesh.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:41.740
So within a target HTTP
proxy, think of this as really

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.710
just a config pointer.

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:48.600
And in this target HTTP proxy,
you'll configure a URL map.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:50.980
The URL map has been
enhanced to allow

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.370
for things like rule
matches and rule,

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:58.480
so really powerful things
that provide granular traffic

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:01.840
routing policies on top of just
looking at the host of URL path

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.982
as part of the L7 mesh.

00:11:04.982 --> 00:11:08.020
The URL map points to
your back end service.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.177
This back end service is global.

00:11:10.177 --> 00:11:12.760
And this is where you're going
to configure things like health

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:15.070
checking, along with
some enhancements that

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:17.740
are part of this launch,
which are traffic policies

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:20.260
that Prajakta will cover later.

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:23.440
In each back end service, you
can configure your back ends.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:26.620
These can consist of managed
instance groups for VMs,

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:30.610
or network end point groups
for containers or GKE.

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.530
So what's really cool
about the solution is

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.840
you don't have to be running
containers or Kubernetes

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:36.730
to take advantage
of this, as long

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:39.220
as you can install a
service proxy on your VM,

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.693
or on your pod, or
on your container,

00:11:41.693 --> 00:11:43.360
you can leverage a
lot of the power that

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:44.830
comes from this platform.

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:47.920
All right.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:51.310
So here we can see a high level
view of the Traffic Director

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:52.360
service mesh.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:53.710
We've shown this a few times.

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:58.840
It's also what maps to our
demo, so it's pretty significant

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:00.740
here.

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:03.910
So what we have here
is in a web service

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:05.740
that's behind our
global load balancer.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:08.767
So we have two back end
managed instance groups.

00:12:08.767 --> 00:12:10.600
They happen to be running
Docker containers,

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:12.058
but they're managed
instance groups

00:12:12.058 --> 00:12:15.010
of VMs deployed in US
central and Asia southeast.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:17.977
So depending on where
that client hits our edge,

00:12:17.977 --> 00:12:19.810
our front end network
is going to route them

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:23.710
to the optimal healthy back
end with available capacity.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.050
From there, we
have a cart service

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:31.357
and a payment service that
is mirrored in each region.

00:12:31.357 --> 00:12:33.190
So what's really cool
about Traffic Director

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:36.670
is we have a global view of
available service endpoints.

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:38.650
And our infrastructure
keeps a map

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:41.560
of what is the RTT
between these end points.

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:43.780
And the default policy
is that we're always

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:46.090
going to route traffic to
the healthiest endpoint

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:47.950
with available serving capacity.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:49.630
So we'll start with
the closest zone

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:54.260
and fail over to regions and
redirect traffic accordingly.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:56.290
Now, I want to
take a minute just

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.060
to contrast this
architecture with how

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.310
you might do this in a
traditional three tier app.

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:02.847
You're going to have some
sort of middle edge proxy load

00:13:02.847 --> 00:13:04.930
balancer and you're going
to configure it on a hop

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:06.423
by hop basis.

00:13:06.423 --> 00:13:07.840
With Traffic
Director, think of it

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:11.860
as embedding a client side
load balancer at every service,

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:14.500
and the policy is also
embedded, as a sidecar,

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:16.400
directly on that service.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:19.822
So once traffic gets
directed to the proxy,

00:13:19.822 --> 00:13:22.030
the proxy makes a really
intelligent routing decision

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:24.543
for the next endpoint.

00:13:24.543 --> 00:13:25.960
And that's a direct
communication,

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:28.252
without having to funnel
traffic through something else

00:13:28.252 --> 00:13:31.730
to make a load
balancing decision.

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:35.320
So here we can see the
view of Maya in California.

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:38.320
I guess I'm Maya today, but
I'll be running the demo here

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:39.550
from the Moscone Center.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.358
So under normal
circumstances, I'm

00:13:41.358 --> 00:13:43.150
going to hit that the
global load balancer.

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:45.370
That's going to direct
me to the web front end.

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:47.590
The web front end is going
to call the cart service,

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:49.673
and then that's going to
call the payment service.

00:13:49.673 --> 00:13:51.700
So we're going to buy
a lot of things today.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:54.400
But under normal
circumstances, default policy,

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:57.820
it's going to route me as
such, through US central.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.660
For Shen, in Singapore--

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:07.240
and I have a client that's going
to emulate a Singapore client--

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:09.100
I'm going to be routed
through the US--

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:11.500
I'm sorry, the
Asia-southeast1 web service,

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:14.920
and then throughout the
mesh in that region.

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:21.660
So what we're also going to do,
and this animation shows this,

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.480
is that we're going to fail
the US-central1 cart service

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:26.440
and observe the behavior.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:28.890
So because Traffic
Director has global view,

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:31.800
and tries to route things
optimally, once the US-central1

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:35.280
service fails, you're going
to see that we get almost

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:37.890
instantaneously directed
to the healthy capacity

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:39.810
in Asia-southeast1.

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:41.310
And then we'll fail
the server back,

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:42.670
and we'll take a look at this.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:45.270
So if we can switch
to the podium.

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:49.990
Very good.

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:53.570
So here you can see we have a
Traffic Director demo project.

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:55.910
This is where the
environment's contained.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:59.508
And I want to show
a few things, so I

00:14:59.508 --> 00:15:01.550
can prove to you that this
is a real environment.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:08.000
So the first thing I want to
look at is our instance groups.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.870
So we have a few.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.500
The first, you can see, is
that we have two web back ends.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:15.630
These are back ends behind
the global load balancer.

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:18.170
This is our web service, and
we have two managed instance

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:19.240
groups of one instance.

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:20.990
I don't recommend doing
a managed instance

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:22.790
group of one
instance, but I guess

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:27.420
it allows us to be fairly frugal
for this demo environment.

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:29.780
Next, I'll call out
the cart service,

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:33.890
which is this managed
instance group here.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:37.340
So that's running as a
VM managed instance group

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:42.110
with Envoy installed on each
in this instance group VM.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:43.880
Finally we have our
payment service.

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:47.240
This is running on
Container Engine, or GKE.

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:49.208
So we have a deployment,
which is our V1.

00:15:49.208 --> 00:15:50.750
And later in this
presentation, we'll

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:53.690
show a traffic splitting
canary style release,

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:57.320
but that's running on GKE.

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:03.080
And we can jump over into
our Kubernetes engine,

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:05.130
and we can take a look
at these workloads,

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:06.440
or these deployments.

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:08.750
Now these manifests have
an annotation on them

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.740
that says "Use network
endpoint groups."

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.140
So if you're not familiar with--

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:13.500
I know I described this
a little bit earlier,

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:14.980
but if you're not familiar
with what network endpoint

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:16.355
groups are, think
of it as a port

00:16:16.355 --> 00:16:20.300
and IP pair that allows us to
directly target the container,

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:21.920
rather than the node.

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:23.720
And why this is
important is, if you're

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:25.763
familiar with how
Kubernetes works,

00:16:25.763 --> 00:16:27.680
there's something called
the kube-proxy that's

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:30.350
almost like a second hop load
balancer, that would then

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:33.270
select a pod behind
that service.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:36.920
This allows us to directly
target the pods themselves.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:39.120
And there's a controller
that gets spun up.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.870
So as pods are scaled up
or down, or they go away,

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:44.270
or new services
are attached, that

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:47.270
is going to program the endpoint
awareness for Traffic Director

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:49.250
to know where to send traffic.

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:51.270
So a little bit
more on that later.

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:53.720
And finally, before we jump
in and start buying things,

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:57.940
we have our network services.

00:16:57.940 --> 00:17:00.860
We have Traffic
Director in the UI.

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:03.490
So here, you can see
that we have our cart

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:06.800
and our payment
back end services.

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:09.530
And they each-- thank
god, they're healthy.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:15.420
But they each have a back
end in Asia and US central.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:17.660
So that's the environment.

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:20.140
So what I'll do first is--

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:21.920
this is a local client.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:24.589
This is me, Mike, in California.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:28.240
And you can see that this front
end is served by US-central1.

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:31.980
So the global load balancer
directed us to the web service

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:34.520
in US-central1, which is good.

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:39.850
The other thing you might note
is that this domain is secured.

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:42.310
Just a little side
note, this is using

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:45.710
a Google Cloud managed cert
on our global load balancer.

00:17:45.710 --> 00:17:47.710
It's also using
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:49.700
So if you're trying
to get to the site,

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:51.250
while I trust
everyone here, I can't

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:53.920
have you stopping the services.

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:55.820
So we're just going
to leave it at that.

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:58.900
So normal connection here.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:00.060
I'm on the web front end.

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:05.190
Let's say we want to
buy a $300 popsicle.

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:06.640
Kind of crazy.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:08.245
We'll add this to our cart.

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:13.190
And you can see now this
changed to say this is

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:15.320
the cart service in US central.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:17.030
And then we'll
confirm the purchase,

00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:18.950
and we can enter our
shipping information.

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:22.640
But it's interesting, I
don't see any shipping price

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:23.570
being calculated here.

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:25.028
Wouldn't it be cool
if we had that?

00:18:25.028 --> 00:18:28.910
So it's a little bit of a
tease to our V2 of payments.

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:31.780
The next view we'll take,
let's buy something as Shen,

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:34.485
in Singapore.

00:18:34.485 --> 00:18:36.110
So you can see that
Shen was directed--

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:39.800
I can refresh this
so you don't think--

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:44.860
So Shen was directed to
Asia southeast region,

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:46.120
being a Singapore client.

00:18:46.120 --> 00:18:48.580
We can get some green slip-ons.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:53.680
And we're directed to the card
service in Asia-southeast1.

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:56.890
And, as you might
guess, also the payment

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:59.500
service in Asia-southeast1.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:02.270
But what would happen
if we failed a service?

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:06.400
So what I'm going to do
is stop the cart service

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.100
in US-central1.

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:11.698
So this is not actually
like turning down the VM.

00:19:11.698 --> 00:19:13.240
It's a managed
instance group of one.

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:14.865
But we're basically
telling the service

00:19:14.865 --> 00:19:16.960
to now return 500
errors that are

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:18.270
going to fail the health check.

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:21.260
So now let's see what happens.

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:25.480
So let's go back to our--

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:27.550
this is me, connecting locally.

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:30.800
So we'll go to
td.gcpnetworking.com.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:31.300
All right.

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:33.532
So we're still going
to the web front end.

00:19:33.532 --> 00:19:35.740
This is, again, the global
load balancer directing us

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:37.150
to the web.

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:42.180
Let's buy a clock, and
we'll add it to our cart.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:44.860
And you'll notice, this is
now served by Asia southeast.

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:47.900
So Traffic Director
had no local capacity.

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:49.468
It's a managed
instance group of one.

00:19:49.468 --> 00:19:52.010
If it had something in another
zone, it would have used that,

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:53.680
but we want to make
this more dramatic

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:55.660
and fail across region.

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:56.470
So we did that.

00:19:56.470 --> 00:20:01.590
And when we confirm
the purchase,

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.890
it stays in Asia, because
the payment service

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:09.550
is the closest next hop, or
endpoint, for the cart service.

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:12.340
So the final thing
we'll do here is let's

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:16.030
start our cart service back up.

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:17.590
This will now allow
the cart service

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:19.390
to pass the health
check, and hopefully we

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:22.720
see everything
stick in US central.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:27.330
So let's buy something else.

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:29.490
Anyone have any preferences?

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:30.970
Goldfish bowl?

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:31.470
What was it?

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:31.970
Radio?

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:33.630
I heard radio first.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:34.920
Where's radio?

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:35.850
Oh, yeah.

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:38.040
It's a bargain at $300.

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:38.630
All right.

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:42.700
We'll add this to our cart.

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:46.150
And the cart, I brushed by
it, but it was US central,

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:48.485
and we stuck on the US central
for the payment service.

00:20:48.485 --> 00:20:50.110
So if we can switch
back to the slides,

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:51.818
I just want to cover
what happened there.

00:20:55.092 --> 00:20:56.320
Oh, cool.

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:57.470
So end point awareness.

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:00.610
How does Traffic
Director and associated--

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:03.910
these service proxies
know where to go?

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:05.920
The way this works for
VMs is we have something

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:07.510
called Back End Manager.

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:09.640
Back End Manager is
informing Traffic Director

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:11.710
of endpoint awareness,
and then that's

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:14.180
getting sent to the
relevant service proxies

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:17.620
so they know where
everything lives.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:22.270
I described this a little bit
for Kubernetes and our GKE.

00:21:22.270 --> 00:21:24.040
The network endpoint
group controller

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:26.680
is talking to Traffic
Director as an annotation,

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:29.200
as part of the
deployment manifest.

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:31.148
And that's how we get
end point awareness

00:21:31.148 --> 00:21:32.440
and we know where the pods are.

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:37.210
So what happened when
something failed--

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:42.290
when I failed that service,
the US-central1 card service?

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:44.720
We're all checking
our end points

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:48.930
as a service, which
is very powerful.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:52.150
And then once the shopping
cart service went down,

00:21:52.150 --> 00:21:55.570
again, Traffic Director removed,
within the service mesh,

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:58.450
that path--

00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:01.150
the shopping cart end point
for US central for that path.

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:04.340
So we're creating this
L7 overlay network here,

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:06.650
which is really cool.

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:10.030
So from the data
plane, what happened?

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:11.762
Again, this is
client side decision,

00:22:11.762 --> 00:22:13.720
so I'm going to show this
from the context of I

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:16.810
came in from the global load
balancer, terminated on the web

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:19.000
service in US-central1.

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:22.000
And via control plane, we
know that that endpoint,

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.940
the healthiest endpoint, the
cart service near central one

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:26.467
was unhealthy.

00:22:26.467 --> 00:22:29.050
So we're going to look at this
from the perspective of the web

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:30.500
service.

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:33.460
So the way this works,
we have our VM here.

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:36.430
We have our proxy
installed, and that's

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:40.720
got a persistent connection
to Traffic Director.

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:46.120
And the proxy is listening on
port 15001 on the local host.

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:49.570
And then the web service
is bound on port 80

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:52.540
for the actual IP
address of the VM.

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:55.750
So the web service wants to
talk to the cart service.

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:58.375
Doesn't know that any service
is down, it just has a fail.

00:22:58.375 --> 00:23:00.430
This Is, again, the
global forwarding rule.

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:01.220
So the app--

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:01.720
I'm sorry.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:05.140
The app, the web service,
requests the cart service,

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:08.360
which is this VIP
that I configured.

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:10.360
And then there's IP tables
rules that configures

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:12.388
netfilter to say anything--

00:23:12.388 --> 00:23:13.930
either everything
that goes outbound,

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:16.630
or specific to the
VIPs in my service,

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:19.330
gets redirected as part
of the output chain

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:21.370
to the service proxy.

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:23.458
And, again, the service
proxy has a configuration.

00:23:23.458 --> 00:23:25.000
It intercepts the
request, and now it

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:26.667
can apply the policy
that was configured

00:23:26.667 --> 00:23:30.580
as part of Traffic Director,
which targets the endpoint.

00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:32.420
So it knows all the--

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:34.190
it's targeting the
healthiest endpoint,

00:23:34.190 --> 00:23:37.120
which actually sends that
traffic over our global VPC

00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:39.710
to Asia-southeast1 endpoint.

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:44.280
So I'll hand it
back to Prajakta.

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:46.450
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Thank you, Mike.

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:46.950
So now--

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:47.550
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:47.550 --> 00:23:51.845
MIKE COLUMBUS: I wondered
if I was going to get--

00:23:51.845 --> 00:23:53.470
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
now a quick warning.

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:55.262
We're going to take
you through a whirlwind

00:23:55.262 --> 00:23:56.218
of different features.

00:23:56.218 --> 00:23:58.760
We obviously won't have enough
time to spend on each of them,

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:00.302
but we wanted you
to know about them.

00:24:00.302 --> 00:24:02.970
And then feel free to get a
hold of us after the talk.

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:06.320
So one of the most interesting
features that Traffic Director

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:07.970
provides is traffic control.

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:09.590
If many of you have
used Envoy, you've

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:11.370
already used these features.

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:12.770
So let's take a quick look.

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:14.420
The biggest thing about--

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:17.930
before, you had to actually go
architect all of these traffic

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:20.090
control capabilities into
either your application

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:21.200
or some system.

00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:23.300
Here, you get all of that
without doing anything

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:24.870
to your application.

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:28.000
So what-- if you think of
how you configure this, think

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:29.170
that there are two buckets.

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:32.420
One is that routing rules, and
one is the traffic policies.

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:35.452
Routing rules look at the
client traffic that's coming in,

00:24:35.452 --> 00:24:37.160
and then they figure
out where to send it

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:38.780
and what to do with it.

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:41.750
The traffic policies are more
about your service that's

00:24:41.750 --> 00:24:44.550
being accessed or
being connected to.

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:46.220
And so what does
that service want?

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:49.550
That's what you express
through traffic policy.

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:53.240
So let's go a little bit
deeper into the routing rules.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.730
Again, reminding you
about the data model

00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:57.650
that Mike showed,
which is you have

00:24:57.650 --> 00:25:00.560
the URL map, that's where
your routing rules reside.

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:03.057
And then you have this notion
of a backend service in GCP.

00:25:03.057 --> 00:25:04.640
And that's where
your traffic policies

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:09.560
reside, because they going and
apply to your back end service.

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:11.525
So what kind of things
would you want to do?

00:25:11.525 --> 00:25:13.150
One of the things
that you'd want to do

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:16.030
is, essentially, imagine
you have a version

00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:18.100
A running of your service.

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:21.130
And you decided to roll
out 2.0 in staging.

00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:23.170
And you want to
actually split traffic,

00:25:23.170 --> 00:25:26.080
because you want to see how
the other new version is doing.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:27.610
That's what you
can seamlessly do

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:29.930
with Traffic Director
and the Envoy

00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:31.430
with what we call
traffic splitting.

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:33.580
So you simply put weights,
like you see here,

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:36.360
and I can split the traffic.

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:39.030
Another interesting thing you
can do is traffic steering.

00:25:39.030 --> 00:25:41.220
So imagine that in
here, you're looking

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.980
at certain characteristics
of your incoming traffic

00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:47.002
and saying, if the
user agent is Android,

00:25:47.002 --> 00:25:48.960
send it to the back end
service that's actually

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:52.175
dealing with Android stuff, and
if the user agent is iPhone,

00:25:52.175 --> 00:25:53.550
send it to the
back end instances

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:56.080
in the service that's dealing
with the iPhone stuff.

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:57.780
And you can do
very rich matches.

00:25:57.780 --> 00:26:00.030
You can also take a
bunch of actions--

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:01.950
traffic splitting, redirects.

00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:04.470
You can do URL rewrites, and
then several other things

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:06.860
as well.

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:09.430
Another very interesting
thing is fault injection.

00:26:09.430 --> 00:26:11.410
So as you write
out your service,

00:26:11.410 --> 00:26:14.112
you do want to test the
resiliency of your service.

00:26:14.112 --> 00:26:16.570
And you want to test it against
things like delays, aborts,

00:26:16.570 --> 00:26:18.130
and so on.

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:21.010
And so, as a part of the fault
injection, what happens here

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:22.690
is that the client,
when it sends

00:26:22.690 --> 00:26:24.782
a request to the
backend service,

00:26:24.782 --> 00:26:26.990
there's a delay that can be
introduced by the Traffic

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:29.590
Director, say on a
percentage of your requests

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:31.960
that are getting sent
to the backend service.

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:33.640
And you can also send--

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:36.100
have certain percentage
of your requests

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:37.360
going there to be aborted.

00:26:37.360 --> 00:26:41.500
So in this way, you can actually
test the resiliency of your app

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:43.900
without really building out
any of this infrastructure

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:46.600
yourself.

00:26:46.600 --> 00:26:48.700
This is one of my personal
favorite features,

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:50.090
which is mirroring.

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:53.650
So what you can do here is
imagine you have your main app.

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:55.630
You can create a shadow app.

00:26:55.630 --> 00:26:58.900
And then as the real traffic
comes to your main app,

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:03.490
you can shadow all of that
traffic to your shadow app.

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:06.173
And then the shadow app treats
this as fire and forget, which

00:27:06.173 --> 00:27:07.840
means it gets the
response, but it's not

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:09.490
going to respond back to it.

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:12.430
So it's a very nice way to
actually go and test out

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:13.960
a bunch of things,
such as binaries

00:27:13.960 --> 00:27:15.490
with production traffic.

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:18.610
Or you can debug errors that are
happening in production using

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:19.840
the shadow debug service.

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:23.693
This is-- I really love
this feature, personally.

00:27:23.693 --> 00:27:25.360
Now let's come to the
second part of it.

00:27:25.360 --> 00:27:27.235
And there are actually
several other features

00:27:27.235 --> 00:27:29.210
that I didn't cover
in the routing rules,

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:30.790
but coming to traffic policies.

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:34.350
Now this is the backend
service telling the client,

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:36.142
here is how I'd
like you to behave,

00:27:36.142 --> 00:27:37.850
or here is how the
traffic should be load

00:27:37.850 --> 00:27:39.740
balanced across my instances.

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:42.720
And so it's from the perspective
of a backend service.

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:44.390
So each of these
policies are obviously

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:47.900
specified for backend service.

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:50.780
Now let's take an example
of one such policy, which

00:27:50.780 --> 00:27:52.230
is load balancing.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:54.360
So Mike showed you the
global load balancing.

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:57.030
So initially, he showed
the global load balancing,

00:27:57.030 --> 00:27:59.690
which Traffic Director
and our global systems

00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:02.330
used to figure out which
is the zone and region

00:28:02.330 --> 00:28:04.130
to send your traffic to.

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:06.830
Now, once it lands there, you
will have multiple instances

00:28:06.830 --> 00:28:07.425
there.

00:28:07.425 --> 00:28:09.800
And so that's when the second
tier of your load balancing

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:10.610
kicks in.

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:12.740
And then it figures
out which is the best,

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:15.770
or the optimal, instance
to use for your traffic.

00:28:15.770 --> 00:28:18.920
So you can see one and two, and
that's what it is referring to.

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:20.630
In the second stage,
essentially you

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:22.790
have several different
LB algorithms

00:28:22.790 --> 00:28:23.730
that you can turn on.

00:28:23.730 --> 00:28:26.750
So things like round robin,
lease request, ring hash,

00:28:26.750 --> 00:28:28.750
random, and several
others, as well.

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:30.250
And you can turn
on session affinity

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:33.580
if that's important to your app.

00:28:33.580 --> 00:28:35.480
So circuit breakers.

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:39.480
How many of you here are
familiar with circuit breaking?

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:41.820
I think it is a topic
we could all talk about

00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:45.750
for an entire day, but my aha
moment about circuit breaking

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:48.270
came when I was talking to
Georgie, who is a distinguished

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:49.713
engineer at Walmart.

00:28:49.713 --> 00:28:51.630
And he said there are
several circuit breaking

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:53.360
solutions that exist.

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:55.710
But one of the really
nice things about Envoy

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:57.900
and Traffic Director
in this solution

00:28:57.900 --> 00:28:59.580
is that it's from
the perspective

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:00.900
of a backend service.

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:02.880
So the back end
service will say,

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:06.390
I can accept a max of five
connections from your client.

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:09.090
And so when the client tries
to send more than that,

00:29:09.090 --> 00:29:11.940
the proxy on the client
just throttles itself.

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:14.370
So it's like back
pressure to the client,

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:17.020
but it's from the perspective
of a backend service.

00:29:17.020 --> 00:29:18.750
And this part was
what was missing

00:29:18.750 --> 00:29:21.160
in several of the other
solutions that existed.

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:24.690
So you had to guess what
the back end service wanted

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:26.118
from a client's perspective.

00:29:26.118 --> 00:29:27.660
So this is the thing
that is actually

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:29.520
huge about circuit breaking.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:31.740
You can also do things
like outlier detection,

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:35.250
and then you can eject out the
instances that actually don't

00:29:35.250 --> 00:29:37.110
conform to whatever
policy it is that you

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:40.620
want for your backend service.

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:42.590
So with that, I
wanted to invite Mike,

00:29:42.590 --> 00:29:44.230
again to give you--
we just picked

00:29:44.230 --> 00:29:46.230
one of the features, which
is traffic splitting.

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:48.175
It is one of our very
popular features.

00:29:48.175 --> 00:29:49.550
And give you a
closer look at it.

00:29:49.550 --> 00:29:50.592
MIKE COLUMBUS: All right.

00:29:50.592 --> 00:29:51.710
Thanks, Prajakta.

00:29:51.710 --> 00:29:53.540
So we get to buy more things.

00:29:53.540 --> 00:29:57.050
So as part of this demo,
we weren't calculating

00:29:57.050 --> 00:29:58.070
shipping costs before.

00:29:58.070 --> 00:30:02.450
We want to deploy a
version two of our payment

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:04.230
that does charge for shipping.

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:10.090
So if we can switch
back to the podium.

00:30:10.090 --> 00:30:12.700
Thanks.

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:16.840
So while Prajakta was
speaking, I did a few things.

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:19.240
And I want to talk
through what I did.

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:25.330
So I am in our Cloud Shell code
editor, which is super helpful.

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:28.540
And I have a few shell
scripts that I ran.

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:32.680
The first was actually creating
the deployment for the version

00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:36.070
2 of the payment service.

00:30:36.070 --> 00:30:41.530
So I just deployed that
and that's running in GKE.

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:45.575
The next thing I did
was I added the--

00:30:45.575 --> 00:30:49.160
let me make this
a little smaller--

00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:53.870
I created the backend service
for our new delivery V2

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:56.870
version of our deployment.

00:30:56.870 --> 00:31:00.350
I created a health check
for it, and I specified

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:02.618
the flag that made it global.

00:31:02.618 --> 00:31:04.910
The next thing I did was I
grabbed the network endpoint

00:31:04.910 --> 00:31:06.800
group name for each cluster--

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:09.866
remember, it was US central
and Asia-southeast1--

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:15.925
and I added that as a backend
to the backend service

00:31:15.925 --> 00:31:16.550
I just created.

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:23.090
So that was creating the
Traffic Director constructs.

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:25.760
And then the final thing I
did was update the URL map

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:30.620
to split the traffic
50/50 across the two

00:31:30.620 --> 00:31:32.068
backend services.

00:31:32.068 --> 00:31:33.610
And what that did
was actually deploy

00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:35.850
this yaml file, which
I called canary.

00:31:35.850 --> 00:31:39.830
So I have a really
simple path matcher,

00:31:39.830 --> 00:31:43.550
and it basically takes the
default service and splits it.

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:46.220
So you can see I have
weighted backend services,

00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:50.480
and I give a weight of 50, or
50%, to the backend service.

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:55.020
This was the V1 version,
and this is the version 2.

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:57.750
And I might as well show it.

00:31:57.750 --> 00:32:00.330
And then, in
Kubernetes Engine, you

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:03.090
can see if I go
into the workloads,

00:32:03.090 --> 00:32:07.320
each cluster now has the
non-delivery version and then

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:11.050
the delivery version of V2.

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:14.140
So let's buy something.

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:16.470
Let's see what we
want to buy now.

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:18.340
Let's buy a surfboard.

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:20.830
It's almost summer.

00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:23.530
So what I'll do is now, when
I add this to the cart--

00:32:23.530 --> 00:32:25.900
again, we're served
by US central cart.

00:32:25.900 --> 00:32:28.570
And you can see now I
have delivery charge.

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:30.490
And this is going to
be fairly variable.

00:32:30.490 --> 00:32:33.340
If I refresh this
page a few times,

00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:35.080
you can see it's about half--

00:32:35.080 --> 00:32:36.820
as you'd expect,
about half the time,

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:38.500
we have the shipping cost.

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:41.740
So basically, what's happening
is the client side proxy,

00:32:41.740 --> 00:32:47.650
when the cart service talks
to the payment service,

00:32:47.650 --> 00:32:49.960
half the time it's choosing
the delivery deployment,

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:51.190
half the time it isn't.

00:32:51.190 --> 00:32:53.000
And I could weight
this accordingly.

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:56.380
So just one example
of how this works.

00:32:56.380 --> 00:32:57.220
But pretty powerful.

00:32:57.220 --> 00:33:00.622
And you might say, well, I
haven't put in my address.

00:33:00.622 --> 00:33:02.080
How does it know
the delivery cost?

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:02.950
It's a demo.

00:33:02.950 --> 00:33:04.990
Maybe that'll be V3.

00:33:04.990 --> 00:33:05.980
So thank you.

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:07.650
I'll hand it back
over to Prajakta.

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:08.720
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Can
we get the slides?

00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:09.553
MIKE COLUMBUS: Yeah.

00:33:14.265 --> 00:33:16.390
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So we spoke
about Traffic Director,

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:18.760
and we spoke about
the side car Envoy.

00:33:18.760 --> 00:33:21.360
We actually have another
interesting product

00:33:21.360 --> 00:33:24.670
called L7 ILB, and I'll talk
a little bit more about it.

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:27.460
If you think of
a lot of you, you

00:33:27.460 --> 00:33:28.960
may be using load balancers.

00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:32.680
And what you're saying is
just give me a simple L7 load

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:36.742
balancer that brings in all
of the capabilities of Envoy,

00:33:36.742 --> 00:33:38.700
but I don't want to deal
with Traffic Director,

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:40.600
and I don't want
to deal with Envoy.

00:33:40.600 --> 00:33:42.370
And because all
you care about is,

00:33:42.370 --> 00:33:44.357
essentially, a load
balancer that's

00:33:44.357 --> 00:33:45.940
for your internal
services that brings

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:47.530
in all of these capabilities.

00:33:47.530 --> 00:33:50.120
And that is what
L7 ILB gives you.

00:33:50.120 --> 00:33:51.730
So think of you've
got a front end,

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:53.020
and then you've got
your shopping cart,

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:54.228
and you've got your payments.

00:33:54.228 --> 00:33:58.510
And you simply put the L7 ILB,
just like any middle proxy load

00:33:58.510 --> 00:34:01.730
balancer, and then you load
balance your instances behind.

00:34:01.730 --> 00:34:04.420
You're essentially not injecting
any proxies on your back end

00:34:04.420 --> 00:34:06.640
instances.

00:34:06.640 --> 00:34:10.239
The other side of it is
there's another set of you who

00:34:10.239 --> 00:34:12.199
have your existing deployments.

00:34:12.199 --> 00:34:15.159
And you want to bring in
all of this service mesh

00:34:15.159 --> 00:34:16.750
and all of these
modern paradigms

00:34:16.750 --> 00:34:18.699
into your existing brownfield.

00:34:18.699 --> 00:34:20.980
And that is the other
use case of L7 ILB.

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:24.070
So it helps you bring service
mesh to your brownfield

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:25.100
without disruption.

00:34:25.100 --> 00:34:26.739
So let's take a look.

00:34:26.739 --> 00:34:29.170
Imagine that you've got
two services, the front end

00:34:29.170 --> 00:34:30.429
and the shopping cart.

00:34:30.429 --> 00:34:31.780
You were able to use--

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:33.969
you were able to put in
are the Envoy proxies.

00:34:33.969 --> 00:34:36.370
You configure Traffic
Director for it.

00:34:36.370 --> 00:34:39.199
Now you have this really
ancient payment service,

00:34:39.199 --> 00:34:42.139
and there is no way you can go
and inject a proxy in there.

00:34:42.139 --> 00:34:43.000
So what do you do?

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:45.219
But you don't want to bring
in all the modernization

00:34:45.219 --> 00:34:48.280
and the capabilities that
you would get with Envoy.

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:49.750
And so what you're
doing there is,

00:34:49.750 --> 00:34:52.030
essentially, you
bring in the L7 ILB,

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:54.730
because it lets you have
all of the capabilities,

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:57.370
but without having to
go and inject anything

00:34:57.370 --> 00:34:58.720
in your back end instances.

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:01.720
So it's a really good way to
start migrating your services

00:35:01.720 --> 00:35:04.060
towards these modern paradigms.

00:35:04.060 --> 00:35:06.190
And this is what it looks
like under the hood.

00:35:06.190 --> 00:35:09.280
So what we did for you is we
abstracted out Traffic Director

00:35:09.280 --> 00:35:12.730
and we abstracted out
the managed middle proxy

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:14.020
pool of Envoys.

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:17.830
And it looks like any other
internal load balancer to you,

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:19.660
and you simply
configure it that way.

00:35:19.660 --> 00:35:22.880
So it's as simple as what you
saw in the previous slide,

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:24.670
but then this is what
is under the hood.

00:35:24.670 --> 00:35:27.460
So we do believe this is a very
good way for you to get started

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:29.230
with the service
mesh, especially

00:35:29.230 --> 00:35:31.570
in your brownfield
deployments, or if you're just

00:35:31.570 --> 00:35:34.167
looking for that next
level of load balancing

00:35:34.167 --> 00:35:35.500
compared to what you have to do.

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:40.030
So we spoke about a
bunch of features,

00:35:40.030 --> 00:35:43.570
but, honestly, that's
just the start.

00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:45.850
We have several exciting
features on the roadmap.

00:35:45.850 --> 00:35:47.590
Some of these we just
started working on.

00:35:47.590 --> 00:35:50.530
Some of these will come
sometime this year or next.

00:35:50.530 --> 00:35:52.360
But one of the first
important things

00:35:52.360 --> 00:35:55.120
we will do an integration
with is all of this security

00:35:55.120 --> 00:35:57.670
features that you get with
Istio, which is things

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:00.280
like mTLS, RBAC, and so on.

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:02.080
The second big
integration we will do

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:04.510
is with a number of
observability solutions.

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:06.170
And we will have more than one.

00:36:06.170 --> 00:36:09.352
So you can-- even today, you
can actually go and integrate

00:36:09.352 --> 00:36:11.560
your own-- like if you're
using, for instance, Apache

00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:13.870
Skywalking, or any of the
other tools, you can go

00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:15.920
integrate those as well.

00:36:15.920 --> 00:36:19.590
The third important thing
is that we always get asked,

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:23.800
so Traffic Director is amazing
for services that are in GCP.

00:36:23.800 --> 00:36:27.130
What do we do with our services
that are sitting on-prem?

00:36:27.130 --> 00:36:29.710
And so that is where we do want
to support hybrid and multi

00:36:29.710 --> 00:36:31.000
cloud with Traffic Director.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:33.730
So your instances stay
wherever they are today,

00:36:33.730 --> 00:36:37.120
but then you use Traffic
Director to go and manage them.

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:39.610
Then the fourth thing is
a lot of you use Istio.

00:36:39.610 --> 00:36:42.220
And so if you're using
Pilot, Traffic Director

00:36:42.220 --> 00:36:45.290
is a very good fit for
a fully managed service.

00:36:45.290 --> 00:36:47.870
So which means we have
to support Istio APIs.

00:36:47.870 --> 00:36:49.540
So you can actually
take the Pilot

00:36:49.540 --> 00:36:51.490
and swap it out with
Traffic Director.

00:36:51.490 --> 00:36:54.490
So that is the whole integration
story into Istio and Anthos

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:57.010
that you will hear more
about in the coming year.

00:36:57.010 --> 00:37:00.790
One of the most interesting
in areas of innovation

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:01.930
is federation.

00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:05.660
This is probably the part where
we've just started thinking,

00:37:05.660 --> 00:37:08.570
but there are other service
mesh control planes out there.

00:37:08.570 --> 00:37:11.800
Some are legacy, some
are totally different

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:12.730
implementations.

00:37:12.730 --> 00:37:15.340
But there needs to be a
way to federate these.

00:37:15.340 --> 00:37:17.740
And you will hear us talking
a lot more about federation

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:21.260
in the coming months,
or coming year.

00:37:21.260 --> 00:37:22.390
So hybrid, I pointed out.

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:23.570
This is what it looks like.

00:37:23.570 --> 00:37:25.820
You've got Traffic Director,
you've got your instances

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:29.170
on-prem, and then you've got the
same Traffic Director managing

00:37:29.170 --> 00:37:30.880
instances that are in GCP.

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:33.230
So that's a hybrid story.

00:37:33.230 --> 00:37:36.120
This is what Istio Anthos
integration looks like.

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:39.550
So you can see that there's
a layer of Istio APIs

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:41.110
that you can use
for configuration.

00:37:41.110 --> 00:37:43.330
And obviously, that should
work whether you instances

00:37:43.330 --> 00:37:46.020
are in GCP or on-prem.

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:47.970
And then the key
benefit of this is

00:37:47.970 --> 00:37:49.860
you will get global
load balancing

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:51.780
for all of your services.

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:53.837
You will get a highly
managed control plane,

00:37:53.837 --> 00:37:55.920
which you don't have to
worry about managing, even

00:37:55.920 --> 00:37:58.163
for your services on-prem.

00:37:58.163 --> 00:37:59.580
And one of the
nice things, again,

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:03.450
is that we use the xDSv2
APIs for communicating

00:38:03.450 --> 00:38:05.580
between the control
plane and the data plane.

00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:07.110
What that means is
you're not locked

00:38:07.110 --> 00:38:08.340
in into Traffic Director.

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:11.130
So you can swap it
out back for Pilot,

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:12.630
or another
implementation, as long

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:17.070
as it uses the open xDSv2 APIs.

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:20.700
So this is what federation
could look like.

00:38:20.700 --> 00:38:23.850
This is probably a place we'll
innovate with the community,

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:24.820
as well.

00:38:24.820 --> 00:38:26.610
And so you will
hear more about it

00:38:26.610 --> 00:38:29.490
in terms of Istio and
several other communities

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:30.780
that we are a part of.

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:32.700
But what is the need
for a federation?

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:35.250
What is driving the need
for us to go look at this?

00:38:35.250 --> 00:38:38.100
First thing is there are
lot of implementations

00:38:38.100 --> 00:38:39.910
where you want a flat mesh.

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:43.320
So you want seamless melding
of what's on-prem and in GCP.

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:45.660
The second one is if you
have Traffic Director control

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:47.550
things that are
on-prem, you should

00:38:47.550 --> 00:38:50.190
assume that the connectivity
between on-prem and GCP

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:51.130
will fail.

00:38:51.130 --> 00:38:53.050
And so you need a
backup control plane.

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.860
So there's a whole aspect
of resiliency there.

00:38:55.860 --> 00:38:57.540
The third one is
sometimes, you want

00:38:57.540 --> 00:38:59.130
to have very controlled
collaboration,

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:01.780
because entity A might be
a totally different setup

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:03.270
from what's in GCP.

00:39:03.270 --> 00:39:06.630
And you want to federate state
things like config identity,

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:08.220
but in a very controlled manner.

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:10.110
And so you need
proper primitives,

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:12.970
interfaces, and abstractions
to make that happen.

00:39:12.970 --> 00:39:14.760
And then the last
one is people are

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:16.920
going to have variety
of control planes.

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:19.170
And as we introduce Traffic
Director, for instance,

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:20.670
we don't expect
people to throw away

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:22.712
all of their control planes
at once, because they

00:39:22.712 --> 00:39:23.590
want to test it out.

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:25.410
And then they slowly
corner introduce it

00:39:25.410 --> 00:39:27.300
into a variety of
their deployments.

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:29.130
And so we do assume
that the world will

00:39:29.130 --> 00:39:32.220
have multiple service
much control planes,

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:35.123
and we have to figure out a way
to operate in that environment,

00:39:35.123 --> 00:39:37.290
and then help you move your
services over to Traffic

00:39:37.290 --> 00:39:37.800
Director.

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:39.925
Or, if you choose to keep
the other ones, that too.

00:39:42.400 --> 00:39:45.270
So what do we have,
overall, in GCP?

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:47.750
We spoke about a
bunch of things today,

00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:50.730
gave you a whirlwind tour of
the Traffic Director and L7 ILB

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:52.630
capabilities.

00:39:52.630 --> 00:39:55.420
So let's, again, go back
to your starting point.

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:58.230
If you are a cloud
load balancing users,

00:39:58.230 --> 00:40:01.710
you can think of Traffic
Director as bringing in Envoy.

00:40:01.710 --> 00:40:06.270
And this could be in the form
of just a managed middle proxy.

00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:08.270
So you use it like any
other load balancer.

00:40:08.270 --> 00:40:11.910
So it's just like an L7 ILB
that's there for your services,

00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:14.610
but with the primitives and
the modern traffic control

00:40:14.610 --> 00:40:18.330
capabilities that you get
with Envoy and with Istio.

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:21.937
Then, if you're one step ahead
in your modernization phase,

00:40:21.937 --> 00:40:24.270
or if you have new apps that
you want to bring in there,

00:40:24.270 --> 00:40:26.550
that's when you would use
Traffic Director with--

00:40:26.550 --> 00:40:28.050
just like with a
traditional service

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:31.080
mesh, where you've got the
Envoy as a sidecar proxy.

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:34.585
And then, the main thing is
that, in both of these cases,

00:40:34.585 --> 00:40:36.960
you don't really have to worry
about managing the control

00:40:36.960 --> 00:40:39.300
plane, because you
actually want to worry

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:41.190
about your apps
and your business.

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:42.930
The last thing any of
you here want to do

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:44.790
is co-manage these
controlled planes.

00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:47.170
So that is what it's
letting you do in here.

00:40:47.170 --> 00:40:49.560
One of the nice things
is that this works out

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:50.940
of the box for
Kubernetes, like I

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:55.800
mentioned before-- self-managed
Kubernetes GKE Docker VMs.

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.940
Because one of the things I hear
very frequently is service mesh

00:40:59.940 --> 00:41:01.260
is for containers.

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:03.300
Actually, that is
not true at all,

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:05.923
because if you took a monolith
and you chopped it up--

00:41:05.923 --> 00:41:07.590
because if you container
has a monolith,

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:09.340
you're not going to
get any benefits.

00:41:09.340 --> 00:41:11.445
So if you're taking a
monolith and chopping it up,

00:41:11.445 --> 00:41:13.320
the first thing you're
actually going to need

00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:16.140
is services management
before you even containerize.

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:18.960
So one of the misconceptions
that we want to remove--

00:41:18.960 --> 00:41:20.420
and also make it true--

00:41:20.420 --> 00:41:22.920
with Traffic Director
is that service mesh

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:25.630
is for [? VNV ?] services
and for containers.

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:28.020
It is for greenfield and
it is for brownfield.

00:41:28.020 --> 00:41:30.340
It is for enterprises,
cloud natives.

00:41:30.340 --> 00:41:35.050
It is an abstraction that
should work across the board.

00:41:35.050 --> 00:41:36.880
And then, of
course, we have gRPC

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:40.170
is another project in
which Google is involved.

00:41:40.170 --> 00:41:42.060
That is an excellent
interface if you

00:41:42.060 --> 00:41:43.680
have apps that support gRPC.

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:46.920
And then most of our testing
for all of Traffic Director

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:48.570
has been with Envoy.

00:41:48.570 --> 00:41:50.640
We really like the
Envoy proxy, because we

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:53.880
believe it delivers almost
like a universal data plane.

00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:57.240
But any other proxy that
supports xDS can be brought in.

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:59.820
There is nothing that locks
you in, either into Traffic

00:41:59.820 --> 00:42:01.170
Director or into Envoy.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:05.110
You can bring your
own Envoy, as well.

00:42:05.110 --> 00:42:07.050
The second part of
it is, so you're

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:10.540
coming now from the
service management angle.

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:12.810
And so that is where,
if you see here,

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:14.840
this is when you
think about an Anthos,

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:17.070
or you think about
GKE On-prem, or you

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:19.230
think about Traffic
Director or Istio,

00:42:19.230 --> 00:42:20.820
what it's really
helping you do is

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:24.600
to deliver hybrid multi-cloud
services at scale seamlessly

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:25.665
without toil.

00:42:25.665 --> 00:42:27.540
And so this is a portfolio
of products that's

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:28.740
going to help you do that.

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:31.210
Imagine that you added
a few more to it.

00:42:31.210 --> 00:42:33.732
And as we integrate
with Anthos and Istio,

00:42:33.732 --> 00:42:35.190
it will be much
more easier for you

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:38.370
to migrate from any of the
things you are using currently

00:42:38.370 --> 00:42:41.370
to Traffic Director or L7 ILB.

00:42:41.370 --> 00:42:44.880
Again, in here, there's nothing
that necessitates Kubernetes,

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:47.640
but on-prem, we have
several customers who

00:42:47.640 --> 00:42:49.470
have brought in GKE On-prem.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:52.230
And then in Google Cloud,
the fastest way to containers

00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:53.820
is using the Google
Kubernetes engine.

00:42:57.240 --> 00:42:58.590
So this is the last part.

00:42:58.590 --> 00:43:00.570
I wanted to--
there is a customer

00:43:00.570 --> 00:43:03.120
we have called [? Mercadi. ?]
So this example

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:04.720
is actually inspired by them.

00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:06.960
This is how they are
thinking of a blueprint

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:10.590
to go and modernize at
their pace without toil.

00:43:10.590 --> 00:43:13.200
So in their case,
they had, essentially,

00:43:13.200 --> 00:43:15.360
a monolith on-prem.

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:18.400
And what they went to
is something like this.

00:43:18.400 --> 00:43:21.220
And you can notice that
there's no monolith on-prem.

00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:23.080
So how did they do it?

00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:25.560
What they did is-- and you
can just follow the numbers.

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:28.230
What they did first
is they, essentially,

00:43:28.230 --> 00:43:30.010
did nothing to the monolith.

00:43:30.010 --> 00:43:32.010
They came to cloud, and
the monolith, of course,

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:33.060
was on-prem.

00:43:33.060 --> 00:43:34.920
They came to cloud.

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:37.090
They put a Google
global load balancer--

00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:39.690
this was for DDOS defense,
this was for TLS termination,

00:43:39.690 --> 00:43:40.620
and so on.

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:42.690
And they put API
gateway instances

00:43:42.690 --> 00:43:44.850
behind the global load balancer.

00:43:44.850 --> 00:43:46.770
So now your traffic
comes in, it seamlessly

00:43:46.770 --> 00:43:49.650
gets routed through the
API Gateway to on-prem.

00:43:49.650 --> 00:43:52.440
So that is the first set
of things that they did.

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:55.020
Then they started chopping
away at the monolith.

00:43:55.020 --> 00:43:57.750
What they did is they took
a chunk of the monolith

00:43:57.750 --> 00:43:59.460
and then they created
service A. So you

00:43:59.460 --> 00:44:01.170
see the number two, there.

00:44:01.170 --> 00:44:03.810
They had a gRCP
interface for it.

00:44:03.810 --> 00:44:06.090
Once they did that, they
actually chopped out

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:08.610
service B. One of the
things you'll notice

00:44:08.610 --> 00:44:11.670
is every time they chopped out
anything from the monolith,

00:44:11.670 --> 00:44:14.220
they created it as a
containerized service.

00:44:14.220 --> 00:44:16.980
So it was created as a
containerized service in cloud,

00:44:16.980 --> 00:44:19.320
instead of doing it on-prem.

00:44:19.320 --> 00:44:22.230
Once they're done
now service B, there

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:25.230
was some services that
could not be move to cloud.

00:44:25.230 --> 00:44:26.550
So what do you do there?

00:44:26.550 --> 00:44:28.200
So they chunked
out the service C.

00:44:28.200 --> 00:44:30.300
You can see the
number four, there.

00:44:30.300 --> 00:44:31.870
And then they put it on-prem.

00:44:31.870 --> 00:44:34.560
But then they used GKE
On-prem to manage it,

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:37.380
because they were using GKE,
which is a Google Kubernetes

00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:39.060
Engine in Google Cloud.

00:44:39.060 --> 00:44:41.700
And so GKE On-Prem gives
you the same primitives,

00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:43.503
but to manage things on-prem.

00:44:43.503 --> 00:44:46.170
And you must have heard a lot of
it in talks in this conference,

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:47.100
as well.

00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:49.200
So now they have GKE
On-Prem managing service

00:44:49.200 --> 00:44:52.230
C. They say, now we are
ready to try out the service

00:44:52.230 --> 00:44:54.120
mesh and some of the
newer capabilities

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:55.830
of the modern paradigms.

00:44:55.830 --> 00:44:59.370
And so they have now put Envoy
proxy on service B and service

00:44:59.370 --> 00:45:02.645
C. This is so that the
services could communicate.

00:45:02.645 --> 00:45:04.770
But now you need a control
plane, because something

00:45:04.770 --> 00:45:07.440
has to go manage those
proxies, and something

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:09.640
has to make sure that
they get configuration,

00:45:09.640 --> 00:45:10.717
and so on and so forth.

00:45:10.717 --> 00:45:13.050
So they first brought in
Istio, because Traffic Director

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:15.000
wasn't available there.

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:17.490
And then Istio
controlled service B,

00:45:17.490 --> 00:45:22.170
and Istio on-prem controlled
service C. Now after this,

00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:26.190
what will happen is now that
Traffic Director is available,

00:45:26.190 --> 00:45:29.130
the Pilot from Istio can be
replaced with Traffic Director,

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:30.640
which is what will happen.

00:45:30.640 --> 00:45:32.970
And then eventually,
when we support hybrid,

00:45:32.970 --> 00:45:35.040
you can see the
arrow six, which will

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:38.040
be able to go control those
services with the proxies

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:39.090
on-prem.

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:41.970
And by this point of time,
because you've chopped up

00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:44.370
all of these services
from your monolith,

00:45:44.370 --> 00:45:48.670
you can see that you've totally
taken away the monolith itself.

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:53.190
So if you see here that it's not
that people just bring in all

00:45:53.190 --> 00:45:55.140
of these technologies one shot.

00:45:55.140 --> 00:45:57.210
They have a strategy,
and they bring it

00:45:57.210 --> 00:45:58.860
in the way that
makes sense for them.

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:00.718
This is just one strategy.

00:46:00.718 --> 00:46:02.510
There's another talk
we are doing tomorrow,

00:46:02.510 --> 00:46:05.230
which describes several
of these strategies.

00:46:05.230 --> 00:46:07.233
But this is a good way
to bring in service mesh.

00:46:07.233 --> 00:46:09.150
And of course, there's
the brownfield strategy

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:14.070
where you could totally use
L7 ILB as your starting point.

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:16.200
So our hope was that--

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:19.500
I know we gave you a whirlwind
tour of Traffic Director, L7

00:46:19.500 --> 00:46:22.140
ILB, how it fits into
the whole ecosystem,

00:46:22.140 --> 00:46:25.950
but our hope is that using
these GCP services, and also

00:46:25.950 --> 00:46:28.380
open source services,
you can manage

00:46:28.380 --> 00:46:30.950
your services seamlessly
and modernized

00:46:30.950 --> 00:46:35.350
from your starting point, at
your pace, and without toil.

00:46:35.350 --> 00:46:36.861
Thank you.

00:46:36.861 --> 00:46:39.663
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:42.470 --> 00:46:43.640
Oh, yeah.

00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:44.960
And one last thing.

00:46:44.960 --> 00:46:46.100
We have a survey.

00:46:46.100 --> 00:46:48.630
We'd really appreciate
it if you filled it.

00:46:48.630 --> 00:46:50.430
We also have an online Dory.

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:52.413
So Dory is where you
can post your questions,

00:46:52.413 --> 00:46:54.080
and then Mike and I
will respond to them

00:46:54.080 --> 00:46:55.498
and post them back online.

00:46:55.498 --> 00:46:56.040
So thank you.

00:46:56.040 --> 00:46:57.300
Thank you for being
here for the talk.

00:46:57.300 --> 00:46:57.900
Thanks.

